decisions:
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-mcp-cli-dep-cycle-duplication
    decision: MCP tools that need cli/lib helpers get intentional duplicate copies in packages/mcp/src/lib/ with header comments explaining the dep-cycle constraint
    rationale: cli already depends on mcp (for tool registration helpers); reverse-importing closes the cycle. Lifting to a shared package (e.g. atelier-timeline) is the right long-term fix; flagged as v1.1 candidate via ~mcp-lib-duplication-vs-cli.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-clip-slug-stable-on-rename
    decision: When media is renamed, the asset KEY (clip-<slug>) stays stable; only .src values cascade. Layer ids never change.
    rationale: clip-<slug> captures provenance at create-time. Renaming the layer id would break any external reference (deltas, parentId from captions). Rename is a media op, not an identity op.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-rename-per-project-mutex
    decision: renameMedia uses an in-process per-project mutex (Map<projectDir, Promise>) to serialize concurrent rename calls
    rationale: Aegis Finding 3 — TOCTOU race between existence check and renameSync was reachable through normal UX (double-click, two browser tabs). Per-project mutex is cheap and sufficient for v1 single-process studio. Cross-process via lockfiles is a v1.1 consideration.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-mcp-workspace-scoping
    decision: atelier_rename_media MCP tool requires projectDir to resolve inside workspaceDir (defaults to process.cwd()) — gate name ^workspace-scoped-project-dir
    rationale: Aegis Finding 1 — the MCP tool accepted any projectDir, opening a tool-misuse vector (prompt injection causing agent to rename in arbitrary dirs). Workspace scoping is defense-in-depth on top of the existing path-traversal basename check.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-image-project-single-image
    decision: kind:image projects hold exactly one image; refuse second image at compose-image-project (use rename or delete first)
    rationale: Multi-image workflows are explicitly carousels. Keeps the kinds orthogonal and stops creators from accidentally creating an ill-defined 'image project with multiple images'.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-state-stub-for-non-video-kinds
    decision: 'Image and carousel kinds scaffold states: { default: { duration: 1, deltas: [] } } rather than making states optional in the schema'
    rationale: Relaxing the schema to make states optional would break 911 existing tests. A single-frame default state is semantically correct for static kinds (renderer draws frame 0) and keeps the type contract uniform.
  - id: TD-2026-05-27-ingest-dispatch-matrix
    decision: Studio drag-drop routes via a 12-cell (file-kind × project-kind) matrix; mismatches return 400 with a clear explanation; uploaded file is cleaned up on reject
    rationale: Explicit routing surface beats silent acceptance. Cleanup-on-reject prevents media/ from accumulating orphan files when the dispatcher rejects.
learnings:
  - 'The 13-agent Paradigm roster (Arky, Kit, Judge, Aegis, Probe, Scribe, Rune, etc.) was sitting unused while I drove a multi-hour refactor solo. User pushed back: this is the opposite of dogfooding. Pattern that worked: Arky decomposes into parallel-safe units → 3-5 Kits build in parallel → Aegis/Scribe/Rune do their specialist passes → Judge + Probe verify → commit. Stored as a [[use-the-paradigm-roster]] memory.'
  - 'Parallel builders WILL collide on shared files (index.ts, .purpose, route dispatchers). Mitigation: explicit block-markers (`// ── v1.0 clip tools ──────`) per slice that builders are told to stay inside. Worked.'
  - The cli → mcp dep direction is permanent. New MCP tools that need cli/lib helpers MUST duplicate (with header comments). Lifting timeline-ops + rename-media + caption-builder + slug-helper into a shared `atelier-timeline` package is the v1.1 architecture move that retires the duplication aspect.
  - Test count estimates by the architect (Arky said target ~1100; actual landed 1023) drift +~10% because builders consolidate test cases into table-driven blocks. Not a problem; just don't anchor on the projection.
  - Aegis security audit before the commit (not after) is the right placement. Found 4 important issues in a fresh-eyes pass that the implementing builder couldn't have flagged for themselves. Three were quick fixes; one was a documentation update.
